In iOS 27.2, Apple has a new settings menu that lets you choose which apps Siri, Search, and suggestions can access, all from one list. Here's what's changed and how to use the new menu.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/Siri-AI-Icon.jpeg" alt="" width="2048" height="1152" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1075526" /><br/>
With iOS 27.2, Apple has replaced the old App Access controls with a redesigned Excluded Apps feature that makes it a lot simpler to keep specific apps away from Siri, Search, and suggestions. <br/>
<br/>
It's actually a bigger change than it might sound. Previously, excluding an app in iOS meant opening Settings ➝ Apple Intelligence & Siri ➝ Apps and adjusting each app's toggles individually. This included a switch that let Siri "Learn from this App," and several suggestion options that appeared in Search and widgets. <br/>
<br/>
What was confusing is that some of these switches controlled on-device personalization, rather than access to an app's contents. Moreover, fully keeping an app's content out of search results also required toggling off a separate "Show Content in Search" switch that was buried in each app menu in Settings ➝ Apps. <br/>
<br/>
Thankfully, in iOS 27.2, Excluded Apps consolidates all of this into a single multi-select list, where you can add an app once and make it unavailable to Siri, Search, and suggestions. Here's where to find it.<br/>
<h2>How to Exclude Apps From Siri and Search</h2><br/>
<ol><br/>
<li>Open the <strong>Settings</strong> app on your iPhone.</li><br/>
<li>Tap <strong>Siri</strong>.</li><br/>
<li>Tap <strong>Excluded Apps</strong>.</li><br/>
<li>Tap <strong>Add Apps</strong>, select the apps you want to exclude, then confirm your selection.</li><br/>
</ol><img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/siri-exclude-apps.jpeg" alt="" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1075922" /><br/>
<br/>
Bear in mind that excluding an app can disable some Siri features for it. For example, the Electronic Freedom Foundation <a href="https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2026/10/victory-you-can-now-exclude-apps-siri-ai">found</a> that excluding Messages stops Siri AI from reading or replying to messages. Also, onscreen awareness – where you invoke Siri and ask it for information about what you're currently looking at – will no longer work in any app you exclude.<br/>
<br/>
<h2>Let an App Back Into Siri and Search</h2><br/>
